ASPIRATIONAL — store scalar widening, TIER 1 REMAINDER (690_020): PLURAL +
MIXED scalar columns. NOT built yet.

Context (690_020/residue.md, Lars 2026-07-05): tier 1 SHIPPED for singletons
(690_021 f64 GREEN — uniform-scalar seeded columns flow the whole write path).
The remainder walled loudly: mixed-type stores + PLURAL column widening =
"typed SoA columns / per-field write events."

Evidence it's scoping not architecture: std/kernel:shape (390_001) runs f64
fields through the SAME transform substrate. Extension, not invention.

Grounded i64-hardcoded sites to widen:
  - store.kz:1056  column array  `{fname}: [{CAP}]i64`  → `[{CAP}]{field_types[i]}`
  - store.kz:1073  row struct    `{fname}: i64`         → `: {field_types[i]}`
  - store.kz:499   single `value_type`                  → per-field types (per-field write)
  - colRead / insert-write / stored-write: thread the column's type, not i64.

Constraint: i64 columns (incl. the [tree] synthesized `parent` self-FK) must
stay i64 and keep working alongside widened columns.

Current failure (SHOWN): a plural `{ n: i64, r: f64 }` cascades to a misleading
"unknown store 'm'" — the i64-only wall fires on `r: f64` in `new`, so the store
never registers and downstream insert/query report it missing. (Bad error msg —
should name the real wall.)

Scalars need NO ownership (a value column never discharges). The reference/string
tier — where `take`/`<std/store:!taken>` discharge finally has bytes to free —
is the NEXT rung, not this one.
